# Release

How a commit gets measured, how the measurement reaches the forge, and how a
measured `dev` becomes a tagged, published, announced `main`. One document
because it is one mechanism seen at three moments: at every push, at the merge,
and on the badge a reader lands on afterwards.

## What a release is

A release is a merge of `dev` into `main`, and every such merge is one. Four
things travel together and a release is half done until all four exist: the
merge commit on `main`, an annotated tag on that commit named for the version,
a GitHub release on that tag whose body is the changelog block, and the registry
publish of the version the manifest names. Nothing is released off `dev`, and a
release tag on any commit `main` cannot reach is a defect the check below names.

The one carve-out is a hotpatch or a small thing that had to reach `main` and
was not worth a version. It is merged without the other three, it is rare, and
it is said to be that when it happens. The next release then carries it.

`0.x` versions bump the way cargo and jsr read them: a breaking change on a
`0.x` line is a minor. The version itself is never inferred; the level is given.

## The gate

The gate is a fixed list of steps run against one commit of one repo, on the
machine of whoever is pushing, and it produces a verdict and a set of numbers.
It decides what a repo is from the marker files at its root, and which files
are markers is the workspace's to say: `[markers]` in `homma.toml` maps a file
name to what it signals, one of `cargo`, `deno`, `nut` or `content`.
`Cargo.toml` signalling `cargo` and `deno.json` signalling `deno` are the two
rows every workspace has without writing them; a workspace adds rows for the
files its other repositories carry, `polka.toml`, `mockspace.toml` or
`nut.toml` say, and a signal outside those four words is refused when the
manifest loads, naming the row. A repo's kind is the set of signals its root
carries: `cargo` adds the cargo calls to a step, `deno` the deno calls, and
`content` and `nut` add nothing, so a repository whose markers say only
`content` or `nut` runs a gate of skipped steps, bar notices, and passes.
`nut` is a nutshell package: it publishes to no registry, since a nutshell
library is imported by its git uri at a tag, but it carries a version,
`[package] version` in `nut.toml`, which a release reads and bumps as it does
`Cargo.toml`'s, where `content` carries none and is never released. A root
carrying `nut.toml` beside `Cargo.toml` or `deno.json` is that manifest's
kind, and its `nut.toml` is not versioned by a release. Which manifests carry
the version is one list per kind, which the plan, the bump commit and the
check's walk over the bumps on `main` all read. A root carrying no declared marker at all is an error,
since that is a repository homma has not been told about, which is a different
thing from one it has been told holds only content. A repo that keeps its
manifests under a subdirectory, `mock/` in this workspace, is read as whatever
its root markers say, `content` where that is all `mockspace.toml` is declared
to mean; the workspace-wide run names such a repo as passed over rather than
skipping it in silence, and reading the manifest under `mock/` for its real
kind is owed. What a step runs per signal:

| step | `cargo` | `deno` | blocks |
|---|---|---|---|
| format | `cargo fmt --check` | `deno fmt --check` | yes |
| lint | `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`, with all features, or once per declared feature set | `deno lint` then `deno check` on every export | yes |
| tests | `cargo test` with all features and with none, or once per declared feature set | `deno test` with the permissions `deno.json` declares | yes |
| deny | `cargo deny check`, only when `deny.toml` exists | nothing | yes |
| docs | rustdoc coverage on nightly, the documented fraction of public items, with all features or on the declared sets | `deno doc --lint` | reports |
| notices | `ante check`, only when `ante.toml` exists | the same | yes |
| description | every published `Cargo.toml` description against its readme's tagline | every named `deno.json` description against the same | yes |

The description step is the one that runs no program: it reads the checkout
and compares two lines of it. The tagline is the readme's blockquote under
the title: the first line beginning `>` between the first heading and the
next heading after it, wherever the badge block puts it and inside a `<div>`
or not, with fenced code skipped, the marker stripped and a quote wrapped
over two lines joined by a space; it is the line a stranger reads first on
the forge. Nothing below the first section is read, so the opening prose is
never taken for it and neither is a quote some later section carries. A
title written setext style, underlined rather than hashed, is a title. The
description is what the same stranger reads first on the registry, and the
manifests read are the ones that reach one: every `Cargo.toml` in the tree
whose `[package]` does not say `publish = false`, and every `deno.json`
carrying a `name`. `target/`, `node_modules/` and `.git/` are left unwalked,
and so is the audit trail, any directory named `research` or `design_rounds`
or ending in `probes`, whose committed crates are evidence rather than
anything a registry sees. Each is held to the tagline of
the readme beside it where one exists, and to the root readme's otherwise,
so a launcher crate under a virtual root is held to the root readme, a
workspace publishing several crates with a readme each is held crate by
crate, and an engine crate nobody publishes is not read at all. The two are
compared with the whitespace at their ends trimmed and nothing else, since a
trailing full stop one carries and the other lacks is the drift being
caught, and a manifest that declares no description fails the same way, a
registry line that says nothing being the same absence as one that says
something else. A readme with no blockquote under its title has no tagline,
and a manifest measured against one is a skip that says so: the gate cannot
compare against nothing, and the readme's own shape is another gate's
business. A tree with no published manifest is a plain skip. The log names
both strings where they differ, and the manifest each pair belongs to.

A step that blocks turns the verdict red on failure. A step that reports puts its
number in the record and the verdict stands. The numbers a run produces are the
test count and how many passed, the documented fraction, the deny advisory count
where it ran, and the wall time of the whole gate; anything a step prints is kept
in the record as its log rather than summarised. The run's one-line summary,
which is also the description on the commit status, names the steps that ran
and how each went; a run in which every step was skipped, which is what a
repository whose markers say only content produces, summarises as nothing to
run and says why, so a green status on such a repository never reads as a
check that happened.

A crate declares the feature combinations it builds under as
`[package.metadata.homma] feature_sets = [["a"], ["a", "b"]]` in its manifest,
and where it does, those sets are the whole of what the gate builds for it:
lint, tests and docs each run once per set, `--no-default-features` with that
set enabled, and `--all-features` is never run for it, since a crate whose
features exclude each other has said which builds are legal and the gate does
not build an illegal one. An empty declaration is refused as a manifest error,
since it would gate nothing and pass. A crate without the entry is built with
all features and with none. On a virtual workspace root, the workspace-wide
runs exclude every member declaring sets, so a plain member keeps its two runs
and a declaring member gets its own against itself; a root that is itself a
package builds alone as it always did, and a declaring member under it gets
its own runs beside that. The docs number is read off the first set's coverage
table. A gate never edits a tree; it runs in the checkout it is given and
fails if that checkout is dirty, because a number measured on a tree with
unstaged changes describes no commit.

## The run record

A gate run lands in the store as a record of kind `gate-run`, which the
workspace declares like any other kind, append-only. Its attributes: `repo` as a
reference into the repos namespace, `sha` as text, `ran_at` as an instant,
`verdict` as text holding `green` or `red`, `steps` as text holding the per-step
outcome and numbers as json, and `log` as text. A number rides as text because
the store's three attribute types are what the records that exist have needed,
and a fourth is added when a kind needs to sort or sum one, which this does not.

The record is what the release reads. `homma release run` looks for the newest
`gate-run` on the exact sha at the tip of `dev`, refuses when there is none or
the newest is red, and never runs the gate itself. So the gate runs once, at the
push, and everything after it trusts the record. A run against a different sha,
however recent, is not evidence about this one.
The sha is asked of the record when step 2 measures it, after the release has set aside a bump a stopped run
left on the local `dev`, and not beforehand from the local tip, which that bump had moved.

## The commit status

Every run posts a commit status to the forge on the sha it measured, under the
context `homma/gate`, with `success` or `failure` as the state and the numbers
in the description in the order the table lists them. The forge api is the same
shape on GitHub and Forgejo, one write per status, and the forge trait grows one
method for it. The token comes from the forge's configured `token_cmd`.

The trunk rulesets on `dev` and `main` require `homma/gate` to be `success` on
the head of any pull request before it can merge, and the workspace's ruleset
applicator carries that requirement. Nothing runs on the remote. The remote is
told that something ran here, and it refuses a merge until it has been told,
which is the whole of how a locally run gate governs a remotely merged branch.

A run made by the `pre-push` hook measures a commit the forge has not heard
of yet, since git sends nothing until the hook returns, so its status cannot be
posted by the hook itself. The hook leaves a poster behind instead: a detached
process, `homma release gate <repo> --post <sha> --await --config <path>`,
handed the manifest the hook ran under as an absolute path, since it inherits
the hook's working directory and nothing else, and the store it has to read
is derived from that path. It asks the forge for the commit, pausing after
every miss, until the forge has it and then posts the recorded status. After
ten minutes of asking it gives up and says so in the record: a
`poster-gave-up` record beside the runs, carrying the repository, the sha,
when it gave up and how long it waited, which is the only surface a process
with no streams has. The forge trait grows the one read that needs, whether a
commit is known: `GET /repos/{owner}/{name}/commits/{sha}` on GitHub, which
answers 422 for a sha it has not received, and `GET
/repos/{owner}/{name}/git/commits/{sha}` on Forgejo, which answers 404. A 404
that is the repository rather than the commit, absent or invisible to the
token, ends the wait as an error rather than as ten minutes of asking:
GitHub's 404 is that outright, and Forgejo's is read as the commit unknown
when the repository answers and as the error when it does not. Posters are
left once every tip has been gated, and only when every tip is green, since a
red tip refuses the whole push and leaves nothing for the forge to be told
about.

A status write that fails because the network is down, or a poster that gave
up, does not turn the run red. The record is kept and the status is posted by
the next run or by `homma release gate --post <sha>`, and until then the forge
holds the merge, which is the correct state for a commit the forge has not
heard about.

## Hooks

homma declares no git hook in its own config and runs no chain of them. A git
hook is an entry of a git-shook manifest, a `shook.toml`, and git-shook runs
each event's entries on its base, `shook`: in name order, stdin replayed where
git feeds one, each entry selected by the paths the event touched where it
names `paths`, and the first refusal stopping the rest and becoming the hook's
own. What homma does is place two manifests where git-shook reads them and
have git-shook register them.

The first is homma's own, named `homma`: the release gate, the `pre-push` row
every workspace has without writing it, so a workspace that declares nothing
pushes gated and one that declares hooks cannot drop the gate by omission.

```toml
name = "homma"

[hooks.pre-push]
run = ["homma", "release", "gate", "--hook"]
```

The second is the workspace's: the `shook.toml` at the workspace root, which
`git_hooks` under `[paths]` names, `shook.toml` where the key is absent. It is
git-shook's format and git-shook reads it; homma reads its `name` and nothing
else, to know what to call the copy, and takes the workspace root's directory
name where it has none, which is git-shook's own rule for a manifest at a root.
A workspace that has no such file registers the gate alone, and the install says
so. A name of `homma` is refused, naming the file, since the copy would replace
the gate.

`homma hook install <repo>` writes both into the member's common git directory
under `shook/manifests/`, as `homma.toml` and `<name>.toml`, and runs `git shook
install --yes --only homma --only <name>`. The operator running the verb is the
consent git-shook asks for, for those two and for nothing that arrived through a
merge. A clone-local manifest runs from the repository root and replaces a
tracked manifest of its name whole, so homma's own tracked `shook.toml`, which
declares the gate under the same name for a plain `git shook install`, leaves
one entry per event and not two. When git-shook refuses or cannot run, each
manifest is given back as it was before the call, the text it had or no file.

The install goes ahead only where git-shook holds the repository's
`core.hooksPath`, its dispatcher directory under the common git directory. homma
never takes that key: it is git-shook's or mockspace's to hold. Anywhere else the
install refuses, says to run `git shook install` in that repository first, and
changes nothing; the refusal is a line and a non-zero exit, so a sweep across a
workspace goes on to the next repository.

An older homma wrote one entrypoint per event into git's own hooks directory,
calling `homma hook run <event>`, and registered `homma hook run <event>` entries
with git-shook. The install removes each such entrypoint that is exactly the text
homma wrote, and git-shook's kept copy of it where git-shook adopted it as
`00-existing`, before git-shook registers the new manifests, since kept it would
run beside the new gate. A file there that is not exactly that text is somebody's
and stays.

`homma hook run <event>` answers only those, while a clone has not been
installed again; an engine can change under a running session. At `pre-push` it
runs the release gate, with git's arguments and stdin, as the old table's first
row did, so a push is never ungated by the move. At every event it first says on
stderr that the hook was written by an older homma and that `homma hook install
<repo>` moves it onto git-shook; at any event other than `pre-push` it then runs
nothing and passes.

`homma hook doctor` says where the workspace's git hooks are declared and
whether that file is there, names a `[hooks]` still in the manifest or an overlay
as no longer read, and names every `homma.*.toml` beside the manifest as read or
as present and not named, since the overlays still carry `[agent.hooks]` and
`[agent.nudges]`. With a repository it also says whether git-shook holds the
hooks path, and names each entrypoint an older homma left in git's own hooks
directory, and it exits non-zero for either. What git-shook registered is
git-shook's to report, `git shook list` and `git shook doctor`.

A manifest or an overlay still carrying `[hooks]` is not refused, since a
running workspace's manifest has one. The parse takes it without the table, and
`homma status` says once under the workspace line:
`  [hooks] is no longer read: the git hooks are declared in <git_hooks>`, then
that each row moves into that file as `[hooks.<event>]` with `run` as an argument
list and `paths` and `{paths}` as before, and the table comes out of the manifest.
The document carries `retired` always, the names of the retired tables present,
empty where there is none.

The order of landings, since nothing moves for a running workspace:

1. git-shook at the revision that carries `shook`, `paths` and `{paths}` is what
   every clone installs; `git shook doctor` names a dispatcher an older git-shook
   wrote.
2. The workspace writes its git hooks into its root `shook.toml` and keeps
   `[hooks]` meanwhile: an older engine runs the table, this one never reads it.
3. This engine lands, and each clone runs `homma hook install <repo>` per member.
   Until then an old entrypoint calls `homma hook run`, which gates the push and
   says to reinstall.
4. The workspace takes `[hooks]` out of its manifest once every engine in every
   clone and container answers
   `homma --output json status | jq -e '.workspace | has("retired")'`.

## The check

Before anything moves, `homma release check <repo>` establishes the invariants
and prints every one it cannot, by id. The set a release needs green is this:

The tags: every release tag is reachable from `main` (`tag.reachable`, tightened
to `main`), on the remote and pointing at the same commit both sides
(`tag.pushed`, `tag.sha.agrees`), and every version a bump commit on `main`
moves to carries one (`tag.bump.tagged`). The tag need not sit on the bump
commit: a version bumped before the repo tagged anything was tagged on a later
commit of that version, and a tag cannot be moved, so what is judged is that
the version was released. The tag counts only where it sits on the bump or on a
later commit of `main`, never on a `dev` commit after it, and a commit two
manifest walks find is one finding; that the tag sits on a commit carrying its
version is `man.version.matches`. That a tag is annotated (`tag.annotated`),
named `vX.Y.Z` or `X.Y.Z` consistently within the repo (`tag.prefix`), alone on
its commit (`tag.dupes`) and fetched (`tag.local`) is hygiene, reported at warn.

`main` holds nothing unreleased: the newest release tag is the tip of `main`, or
what lies between is a hotpatch named as such (`main.unreleased`, the one id
the catalogue did not have, since it never read `main`). The manifest version at a tag
equals the tag (`man.version.matches`), and the working version is above the
highest published one and its smallest legal successor at the level given
(`man.current.forward`, `man.current.smallest`).

The registry: published versions ascend in publish order (`order.ascends`),
skip no number (`semver.gaps`; a pre-release of a legal next version, a later
pre-release of the same version and its release are each one step), every published version has a tag
(`reg.orphan`), the newest tag has a published version (`reg.unpublished`,
which blocks on the newest tag and warns on older ones, since a tag from before
the package was ever published is history and not a release nobody finished),
and where a package ships on both jsr and npm the two hold the same set
(`both.sameset`).

The tree: clean, on a branch, nothing unpushed, no untracked file
(`tree.clean`, `tree.attached`, `tree.pushed`, `tree.untracked`), and no
workflow file anywhere in the tree (`hist.workflow.tree`).

The rest of the catalogue is reported at `warn` and does not block, each row by
the id it had before. A yanked version was published and keeps its slot, since
no registry publishes a version twice, so the registry layer keeps the yanked
versions jsr marks apart from the live ones, and `reg.unpublished` counts a
yanked version as published.

The branches and the remote: commits on the remote's copy of the branch that are
not here (`tree.uptodate`), a stash nobody applied (`tree.stash`), a second
worktree still checked out (`tree.worktrees`), a trunk that is the release line
itself (`branch.trunk`), a remote branch merged into the trunk and still there
(`branch.merged`), one carrying commits the trunk lacks, with how many and when it
was last touched (`branch.unmerged`), a local branch whose upstream is gone
(`branch.orphan`), and a `pushurl` on the remote, printed with whatever sits
between `//` and `@` hidden, since those carry tokens (`remote.pushurl`).

The tags and refs: a tag that names no version (`tag.stray`), a version the
manifest was set to at more than one commit of the timeline below
(`tag.bump.once`), version tags whose commit dates run against their version
order (`tag.order.matches`), and `refs/original` or `refs/replace` left behind by
a rewrite (`ref.filterrepo`).

The history: a workflow file anywhere in the history (`hist.workflow.past`), a
tracked file at `HEAD` carrying a GitHub or Slack token shape
(`hist.secret.tree`), a `.env` or `secrets/` ever committed (`hist.env`), a blob
at `HEAD` over a megabyte (`hist.large`), a file carrying an opening, a divider
and a closing conflict marker in that order (`hist.conflict`, where a row of `=`
alone is prose and no conflict), and a commit message carrying a generated-with
byline (`hist.byline`).

What a reader lands on: a README and a licence tracked at the root
(`doc.readme.exists`, `doc.licence.file`), and no em-dash in a tracked Markdown
file (`doc.emdash`).

The sequence: a tag that reads like a version and is not one (`semver.parses`),
version tags that skip a number (`semver.gaps.tags`), a gap in what a registry
holds that no tags could close (`semver.gaps.closable`), and a tagged version a
registry skipped while holding a later one (`reg.sandwiched`). A step is legal
as `semver.gaps` reads it, one definition for the check, the version timeline
and the estate; a gap is closed by the releases one step after another between
its two ends, and a gap nothing closes is told apart from no gap at all.

The rows that read a built bundle or a tarball, the jsr backfill, and the rows
that need a forge listing are in the backlog.

## The run

`homma release run <repo> --level <patch|minor|major>` is the release, and the
level is required: a run without one refuses, and the tool never reads a level
off the commits. `--version <X.Y.Z[-pre]>` stands in for it, exactly one of the
two, and names the next version outright, which is how a pre-release is cut,
since no level makes one. It must be one step above the last tag in the sense
`semver.gaps` reads, or for a first release the manifest's version or one step
above it, and a manifest already at it is the release as with a level. It names
one repo's version, so a run naming no repo refuses it. A level taken from a
pre-release makes that pre-release's release: a patch from `0.0.5-rc.1` is
`0.0.5`, a minor from `0.1.0-rc.1` is `0.1.0`, as cargo-release reads it. What it does, in order, stopping at the first refusal:

1. Runs the check and refuses on any blocking finding.
2. Finds the newest green `gate-run` for the tip of `dev` and refuses without it. The tip is the one this step sees, after a bump a stopped run left on the local `dev` is set aside, so a run recorded on the tip the gate measured is found beside a leftover local bump.
3. Prints the plan: the two branches it carries, `dev` onto `main`, or `main`
   alone where the repo has no working branch here or on the remote; the
   commits since the last release tag with their prefixes,
   the version the level makes of the last tag's, the changelog block those
   commits become, and what publishes where, in dependency order. A manifest
   already at that version is the release and nothing bumps; a manifest at any
   other version above the last tag disagrees with the level, and the plan
   refuses with both numbers rather than picking one. With `--dry-run` it stops
   here.
4. Bumps the manifest on `dev`, prepends the block to `CHANGELOG.md`, commits
   both as one commit, and pushes `dev`. A `dev` that takes the push gets the
   push. Where the forge refuses it outright, a ruleset that takes changes to
   `dev` only through a pull request, the run lands the same commit through one
   itself: the commit is pushed as `release/<repo>-<version>`, and the pull
   request from it into `dev` is merged with a merge commit, never a squash, so
   the bump stays an ancestor of `dev`. The run waits for that merge, reads
   `dev` back from the remote, and takes it once it is a merge of the bump onto
   the `dev` the gate measured with the tree the two make together; the clone's
   `dev` becomes that merge, and the branch is deleted. Another merge landing
   on `dev` in between stops the run with what the forge holds, nothing tagged.
   A bump a `dev` already carries is not made again: a manifest at the planned
   version and a `CHANGELOG.md` already holding a heading for it get no commit
   and no push, and a missing half, the block or the version, is written alone.
   The block's own text is never compared, since the commits since the last tag
   include the bump itself once a run is resumed.
5. Merges `dev` into `main` with a merge commit, never a squash, never a
   fast-forward, so the tag has a commit of its own and `main` keeps every
   constituent commit. Where the forge refuses the push of `main` outright, the
   local merge is undone and the release pull request does it instead, `dev`
   into `main`, merged by the forge with a merge commit under the same subject
   at the head the plan measured; the clone's `main` takes that commit, and it
   is what step 6 tags, once it is checked to be a merge of `dev`'s tip onto
   where `main` was, with the tree the two make together, so a merge the
   forge resolved another way is never tagged.
6. Creates the annotated tag on the merge commit and pushes it. Where the
   repo's forge is GitHub and the workspace manifest names `forge_route` under
   `[paths]`, the command every tag, release and remote branch deletion of the
   workspace goes through, the tag is asked of it instead,
   `forge_route tag <repo root> <tag> <sha> <tag>`, which tries each route the
   machine has (`gh`, git, the console's forge Worker) and takes a tag the
   remote already holds on that commit as made, so a cloud session whose proxy
   refuses a tag push still tags. homma never mirrors that order; the command
   keeps it. Either way the clone then fetches the tag from the remote and
   refuses where it names another commit than the one tagged, so the tag a
   route made on the remote is in the clone too, and the next plan starts from
   it rather than planning the same version again.
7. Creates the forge release on the tag with the changelog block as its body.
   Where the route took the tag, the release is asked of it too,
   `forge_route release <repo root> <tag>` with the block as `FR_NOTES`, and a
   release that exists is made already; the command makes a pre-release that
   is never the latest for a version with a pre-release part, and the latest
   for a stable one.
8. Publishes to every registry the manifest names, in dependency order across
   the repos the plan named, waiting for each registry to serve a version before
   the next package that depends on it publishes.
9. Writes the badge json to the `badges` branch.

Before step 1, and before any registry is asked, a run reads the history it
would release. Where the workspace manifest names `history_scan` under
`[paths]`, homma runs it with the repo's root as its one argument, for each repo
the run would release and with `--dry-run` too. Exit 0 is a clean history and
the run goes on. Any other exit refuses that repo's release with nothing moved,
with what the command printed and the one remedy: the repository is rehoused
onto a clean history, which is a human's decision. A command that could not
scan refuses the same way, since a scan that did not happen reads like a clean
one. The command is the workspace's, as `forge_route` is: what counts as an
agent's byline, a tool trailer or a tool's advert, and which of them a
workspace permits, belong to it, and homma keeps no copy. A manifest that names
a `history_scan` that is not there refuses the run before anything, as a
`forge_route` that is not there does. Unset, nothing is scanned.

The forge writes of steps 4 to 7 are one contract, `ReleaseForge`, with its
`tag`, its `release`, its `land`, a pull request from one branch into another,
merged with a merge commit at a given head, and its `drop_branch`, the branch
a landed pull request came from deleted. `land` is the release pull request of
step 5 and the bump's of step 4 where `dev` refuses the push. `ByClient` tags
with git in the clone, pushes the tag and makes the release through the forge
client, and has no pull request: a refused push stays refused, with the reason,
and a bump the forge refused for `dev` stays on the local `dev` for the rerun.
`ByRoute` asks `forge_route` for all four, `land` as
`forge_route pr <repo root> <head> <base> <title> merge <sha>`, which takes
the pull request open from the head into the base or opens one with no body,
and merges it at that sha with the title as its subject and no message. That
verb is the same mechanism the workspace's landing scripts use, and homma
keeps no copy of it. A refusal of the release line's push is read off git's
`[remote rejected]`, as those scripts read it; any other failed push is the
error it was. `drop_branch` is `forge_route delete-branch <repo root> <branch>
<sha>`, which queues a branch the forge refuses to delete now for the
workspace's sweep, and a deletion that fails even so does not stop the run.

A run that stopped after step 4 made its commit is resumed by running it again.
Before step 1 the run fetches the trunk and the release line, and reads a bump
as a commit with one parent whose subject is `chore: release <version>`, or as
the merge commit of a pull request that landed one: two parents, the same
subject, and a second parent that is such a bump. Where the remote's `dev` is
that merge, whether the run's own pull request or another's landed it, with the
tree the bump and the merge's first parent make together, the bump is the
commit made on that first parent, so the block it holds covers every commit the
release carries, and the clone's `dev` is at the bump, at the merge or at the
merge's first parent, the trunk is set back to the first parent for the check,
the gate run and the plan, and step 4 takes the merge as it is, with no commit
and no push. A merge of a bump made on another commit than the one it was
merged onto, a `dev` that moved under the pull request, is none of these: its
block leaves out the commits that moved `dev`, so nothing is tagged and the
forge's `dev` is left for a human to settle. Where
the trunk is one commit past the remote's, and that commit is a bump on the
remote's tip, the run sets it aside, so the check, the gate run and the plan see
the commit the gate measured, and step 4 takes it back instead of writing a
second. Where the remote's trunk already holds the bump as the tip, the push
made and the run stopped before the tag: the trunk is set back to the bump's
parent the same way, and step 4 takes the bump without a second push.
Where the remote's release line has, on its first parents, a merge whose
second parent is that bump and whose tree is the bump merged onto the release
line the clone holds, the one the run saw, the release pull request merged
too, and step 5 takes that merge and tags it. A merge of the bump with any
other tree is refused before any pull request, nothing tagged, and is left for
a human to settle. A bump some tag already contains is released and
is none of these. A plan for another version than the bump carries refuses,
and so does any step before 4; either way the trunk is put back where it was.

Where the forge's release line after the release pull request is not what the
release asked for, another merge landing between the push and the merge, the
run stops and says what the forge holds: nothing is tagged, and a rerun refuses
the same merge again, since its tree is not the one the gate measured. A bump
that cannot be put back is reported together with the refusal that stopped the
run.

The pre-push gate measures each tip it is handed and records the run. A tip the
record already holds a green run for is not measured again in the hook: the
run is the gate's answer for that commit, and a landing whose push is retried
over a moved `dev` pays the gate only for the commits it made new. A gate run
by hand always measures.

The engine picks one per repo: `ByRoute` for a GitHub repo where the
manifest names the route, since the route reaches GitHub alone, and `ByClient`
for every other. A manifest that does not read as a workspace's is an error
rather than no route, and so is a route it names that is not there, both read
before any repo runs, because falling back to the git push is the refusal the
route is there to avoid and a route that cannot run fails at step 6, each after
`main` has moved. The fetch after step 6 compares the remote's tag before it
writes it into the clone, so a tag the remote holds on another commit is
refused without leaving the clone a version tag on the wrong commit.

A cargo workspace publishes its members in dependency order, one `cargo publish`
per member. A deno package publishes with `deno publish`, and where it also ships
on npm, builds the npm package and publishes that too. The registry tokens come
from the same credential tool the forge token does.

`homma release run` without a repo releases every repo the workspace names that
has something unreleased, with one `--level` for all of them, which is the
shape a docs pass across the estate takes. A repo with nothing unreleased is passed
over with that said; one whose manifest is off the level, or whose next version
is no single step above its last tag, refuses the sweep, since passing it over
would leave it unreleased without a word. The order is by name today, and a
run whose set carries a dependency edge, one repo it would release naming
another as a git or registry dependency, is refused with the two names, so the
dependent is released after its dependency by two runs by name. Dependency
order across repos is owed, the way the publish already orders the members of
one workspace.

## A virtual workspace

A cargo workspace whose root names no `[package]` and no
`[workspace.package] version` has no version of its own: each member carries
its own and moves on its own. Such a repo releases as a set, and the set has a
primary, the member named as the workspace manifest names the repository, never
as the clone's directory happens to be named, or the one publishable member
where there is one. Any other shape refuses the plan with the names rather than
picking one, and the check reports it (`man.primary`).

The primary's version is the release's. The level, or `--version`, makes its
next version out of its last tag, step 4 writes it into the primary's own
manifest, and the tag is named by it in the repo's convention, so a repo whose
tags were the primary's versions keeps them so. Every other publishable member
rides in the release that carries it: its version is its manifest's, which a
commit on `dev` bumps by hand. It moves where its version at the head differs
from its version at the last tag, and it moves one step as the primary does,
or the plan refuses with both numbers. A member whose files changed since the
last tag while its version did not is named in the plan as a warning, since its
change would not publish. A member that moves publishes and the block
names the move on a line of its own, and a member that stays publishes nothing.
A riding member has no tag of its own. Its version is tagged by the release tag
on the commit of `main` that bumped it.

The check reads each crate against its own versions. The primary is read
against the tags as a single crate is, both through the root manifest from
before the repo became a workspace and through its own manifest since. Every
other member: each bump of its manifest on `main` lands on a commit a release
tag points at (`tag.bump.tagged`), each version it has on the registry is one
such tagged bump (`reg.orphan`), the version it carries at the newest tag is
published (`reg.unpublished`), its working version is not below its published
one (`man.current.forward`), and its registry versions ascend and skip nothing
(`order.ascends`, `semver.gaps`).

## The version timeline

`homma release versions <repo>` reads a repo's version history off its commits,
since a release is a commit that changed the version and a tag is a separate act
somebody has to remember. The timeline is every commit of the trunk and the
release line, here and on `origin`, that touches the manifest, oldest first by
ancestry and never by the clock, with the points where the version changed; a
version that only ever lived on a feature branch is no release. The manifest is
the root `Cargo.toml` where it names a version, else a virtual workspace's one
member carrying a version of its own (one inheriting the root's is the root's
line), else `deno.json`; several members carrying one is refused with their
names, and `--manifest` names the one meant.

A step is clean where it is one legal increment as `semver.gaps` reads it, and
the design phase, `0.0.0-dNN`, advances by one, leaves at `0.0.1` and nowhere
else, and is never gone back into. A tag counts for the last version change it
reaches by ancestry, since the release tags the merge that carries the trunk and
not the commit that bumped it, and only a tag naming a version counts.

`versions plan <repo>` lists the changes no tag released, leaving out `0.0.0`
and anything at or before a step backwards, which is scaffold that never
shipped; the steps that are not clean, with the legal ones they skipped; and per
crate the repo publishes, what crates.io holds against the manifest's version.
`versions tag <repo>` tags each of those at the commit that set it, in the repo's
own tag convention, bare (`0.1.0`) where it has no tag yet, as this workspace's
repos tag, saying so in the message. `versions retag [<repo>]` lists the
tags no trunk reaches and where each belongs: a trunk commit with the same tree,
which is proof, else one with the same author date and subject, which is
evidence and said to be; `--apply` moves them, refusing where more than one
commit matches, and appends the git command that undoes the move to
`retag-journal.txt` in the repository's git directory before the tag moves,
since a tag move leaves no reflog. Neither verb pushes: the tags are made here
and go out through the forge route.

## The estate

`homma release estate` measures every package the workspace's repositories
publish: each cargo crate not marked `publish = false`, a member inheriting the
root's `[workspace.package] version` at that version, and the jsr package a
`deno.json` names where its scope is `[registries.jsr] scope`, or any scope where
none is set. Only directories that are repositories are read. Per package: its
tags, jsr reading `v`-prefixed ones and cargo bare ones; what its own manifest
said at each tag, read out of the tag and never the tree; what the registry
holds, asked live through the same registry layer the check uses; the tagged
versions the registry holds neither live nor yanked; the jumps in what it holds;
and the tags whose manifest disagrees. `estate jumps` and `estate missing` show
one column each, and `--json <path>` writes the whole measurement; the two
together are refused with a reason and nothing is written. A registry
that cannot answer stops it with its reason rather than reading as nothing
published.

## The changelog

`CHANGELOG.md` lives on `main` in every released repo, newest first, one block
per release, kept forever. A block opens with the version and the date and
carries the commits since the previous tag grouped by their conventional prefix,
`feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `chore`, `test`, in that order, one line each
in the commit's own words with its short sha, and the pull request number where
the merge commit carries one. Commits with no prefix land under `other`. The
same block, verbatim, is the GitHub release body. There is no summary paragraph
and no prose the tool writes on its own.

## Badges

Each repo carries an orphan branch named `badges` holding one json file per
measured thing, in the shape shields' endpoint badge reads: a schema version, a
label, a message and a colour. The files: `tests.json`, `docs.json`,
`deny.json` where deny ran, and `gate.json` for the verdict, plus `version.json`
naming the version that shipped. The release rewrites the branch on every run,
so a badge shows the number the gate measured for the commit the tag points at.

A readme references one as
`https://img.shields.io/endpoint?url=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/<owner>/<repo>/badges/<file>`.
The branch is fetched by every clone, a few kilobytes and never checked out,
and that is accepted over a ref outside `refs/heads/` that raw file hosting
cannot resolve.

## What a release reaches

Two things decide whether the release verbs work at all in a cloud container, and both are settled by what they read.

The registry indexes and the forges are asked over TLS against the system's roots, and where `SSL_CERT_FILE` or `SSL_CERT_DIR` is set, against the roots it names. They are not asked against a store of roots compiled in. A cloud container sends every request through a proxy that signs with its own authority and names it only through that variable, so a compiled-in store refused every request as an unknown issuer and no release got as far as looking at its repository. The roots are read once for the process, in one crate-private place, and the registry requests, the GitHub client and the Forgejo client all start their agent from it, so what they trust cannot differ between them. A test over the crate's source names any file outside that place that makes an agent by a way the test does not list as allowed, and the agent type is only re-exported from that place, so any `ureq::Agent` written outside it is reported in whatever form it takes. A store that ends up with no root in it makes every request fail as an invalid peer certificate, which says nothing of the file that was missing, so the cause is written once to standard error then: a heading, a line for each of `SSL_CERT_FILE` and `SSL_CERT_DIR` that is set, and whatever the loader said.

Every release verb finds its repository among every clone under the workspace root, whether the repository is named or is the one the working directory is in, and whatever `repos` the local file names. Which repositories a clone is for says nothing about which can be released from it, and a coordinator in a clone for one body of work releases the others. The one verb that names no repository and means all of them, `release run` with none, stays within the scope, since a container attaches clones it only reads and a sweep over every clone would try to release those.

## Credentials

The forge token comes through the forge's `token_cmd` or `token_env`, as every
forge call already does. The registry tokens come through the same command with
the registry as its argument (`crates-io`, `jsr`, `npm`), and the publish steps
hand them to `cargo publish`, `deno publish --token` and an npm user config
written for the call, never to the environment of the shell that ran homma.

`homma release auth` is where those tokens are kept valid before a release. Four
credentials, a fixed set: three token registries, crates.io, jsr and npm, whose
token is held in a store and proven by calling the registry's own identity
endpoint with it, and github, a cli registry whose credential `gh` keeps and whose
check and fix are `gh`'s own, so nothing is copied out of it. A check calls
something live every time and asks about the token held, never about whatever
the machine's own configuration points at.

How each answer reads: no answer is unreachable, which says nothing of the token
and prompts for nothing; 401 is a rejection; crates.io's 403 is a token scoped to
publish, which cannot read the endpoint and is the scope to mint, so it is
accepted unnamed; 200 is accepted, naming the holder. An accepted token is graded
by its recorded expiry: none is fine, more than two weeks out is fine, two weeks
or less is expiring and gets walked through, past is invalid. crates.io takes the
token bare and the other two as a Bearer.

The store is the keychain on macOS, one generic password per registry, service
`<prefix>-<name>` with the prefix `[auth] service_prefix` gives, account `publish`, the expiry in its comment, through the
`security` command. On any other system it is the store homma already reads
secrets from: the variable `[registries.<name>] token_env` names, read and never
written, with no expiry, and a write refused by a reason that names the variable.
Never the registry's `token_cmd`, which the workspace may point at this very verb.
A store that cannot be read, a keychain program that is not there included, is a
reason on that credential's line: the status still prints every line and exits
non-zero, and nothing is asked of the registry.

A store that refuses writes is found out before a paste is asked for: the walk
for that credential stops there, naming the refusal and so the variable, and the
credential counts as needing work, as does a proven token the store then refuses
to take. `auth`, `auth fix` and `auth status` exit non-zero for either. A stored
secret never appears in a `Debug` rendering.

A token is proven before it is stored: a pasted token is judged as the candidate,
and only one the registry accepts reaches the store, three pastes at most. The
npmrc npm reads a token from is written at `[auth] npmrc`, against the workspace
root where relative, else `homma/npmrc` under the user's cache directory, never
inside a repository; it is rewritten whole every time, so a rotated token leaves
nothing under it, and created readable by its owner alone before anything is in
it. Its line is the one the publish writes.

```
homma release auth                       check all, walk through what needs it
homma release auth status                check only; non-zero where anything is off
homma release auth fix <name>            walk one through, even where it passes
homma release auth forget <name>         drop a stored token
homma release auth expiry <name> <date>  correct a recorded expiry, `none` to drop it
homma release auth token <name>          print one token, bare, for a publish
homma release auth npmrc                 write the npmrc and print its path, bare
homma release auth env                   the export lines a publish script wants
homma release auth diagnose <name>       the token masked, and what the registry said
```

`--no-browser` prints a mint page's url rather than opening it. Prompts go to
standard error, so `token` and `npmrc` print their answer alone for a pipe.

## The surface

```
homma release check [<repo>]              the invariants, by id, blocking ones first
homma release gate [<repo>] [--sha <sha>] run the gate now, record it, post the status
homma release gate --hook                 what the pre-push entry runs
homma release gate --post <sha>           post a recorded run's status again
homma release gate --post <sha> --await   the same, once the forge knows the commit; the hook's poster, with `--config`
homma release plan [<repo>] --level <l>   the plan and nothing else; `--version <v>` for a level
homma release run [<repo>] --level <l>    the release, or every unreleased repo
homma release badges <repo>               rewrite the badges branch from the newest record
homma release auth [<verb>]               the publish credentials, proven live; the verbs under Credentials
homma release versions [<repo>] | --all   the version timeline; `plan`, `tag`, `retag [--apply]` beside it
homma release estate [jumps|missing]      every package against its tags and registry; `--json <path>`
homma hook install <repo>                 place homma's gate and the workspace's shook.toml as clone-local manifests and have git-shook register them, or refuse where git-shook does not hold the hooks path
homma hook run <event> [args...]          what an entrypoint an older homma wrote calls: the gate at pre-push, and a line saying to reinstall
homma hook doctor [<repo>]                where the git hooks are declared, a retired [hooks], the overlay files, and with a repository whether git-shook holds the hooks path and what an older homma left
```

Every subcommand prints for a human when a terminal is attached and its result
alone when one is not, the split the root design decides for the whole surface.
